Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.470 align:start position:0%
 
today the supreme court heard one of the  biggest reproductive rights cases in  years the justices deliberated over a  mississippi abortion ban after 15 weeks  of pregnancy their ruling on  mississippi's law could have  far-reaching ramifications beyond the  state after about two hours of hearing  arguments justices indicated all six  conservative justices indicated that  they would go with allowing states to  roll back abortion access so we want to  bring in jennifer poppeck to talk about  today's hearing and what it means in the  ongoing abortion battle battle jennifer  is the legislative director for national  right to life committee jennifer welcome  to quick take  what is your reaction to this news after  we're now hearing that the conservative  members of the court are uh potentially  going to vote in favor of rolling back  abortion access  sure i would say from the point of view  nationwide to life we're very encouraged  by what we heard today  now it's difficult always to know for  sure just based on oral arguments alone  you know what sort of outcome we're  going to get but we're encouraged i  think based on the questions  you know there's a majority of justices  who seem likely to in some way either um  you know explicitly overrule the roe  versus way decision as modified by casey  or to you know do something a little a  little uh narrower at least allowing um  reach like you know more pro-life laws  at an earlier stage than viability so  either outcome i think we're encouraged  so tell me a little bit about the  viability question here does does your  organization uh feel that the viability  should be  changed should be addressed by the  supreme court and if so how likely do  you think that is given that you know it  would be really unprecedented for the  court to overturn something like roe v  wade they've never done something like  that before  no that's that's correct i mean it's  been you know 30 years since the last  sort of landmark case which was the  casey decision  and there's just been you know a real  pattern here although we have this undue  burden standard that was sort of  governing and saying you can't put an  undue burden way of a woman prior to  viability  it's you know it's it's left a situation  where it's very difficult to pass laws  to protect unborn children so we're  certainly in favor of moving that back  and giving states you know state  legislatures passing laws working  through their own process to say hey  this is a point at which we want to  protect the baby maybe it's the baby can  feel pain maybe it's the baby you know  has a heartbeat and brain waves  there are other places at which the  state might come in and say we have a  right in the life of this unborn child  you know and and be able to pass more  meaningful restrictions sure but but at  15 weeks even if a fetus was given a lot  of life-saving efforts from doctors and  experts uh it would be really hard for  that fetus to survive so so how do you  kind of defend that argument  sure you know the mississippi law itself  does have a lot of exceptions for help  of the mother um life mother that sort  of thing we think this is a this is a  very developed baby yes it is  not able to fully survive on its own  even you know the earliest preemie  babies are surviving at 21 22 weeks  so  you know this is a very late stage of  pregnancy this is a very developed baby  this is one that has met a lot of you  know physical milestones and a baby  that's likely to survive and um you know  if the pregnancy continues so this this  is a stage you know at which  i think a broad swath of the american  people would say  you know there might be some different  opinions on abortions early in pregnancy  this is a very late stage of pregnancy  i want to talk about the state angle  here because i know that your  organization and the conservative  justices are leaning uh now that we know  how they're reacting to these arguments  they're leaning towards allowing states  to decide on this issue  what do you envision that looking like  for the country moving forward it would  seem that the south would pretty much  enact bans on abortion and the north  then more liberal states wouldn't do  that what do you think that that does to  the makeup of the country in the  electorate  you know sure and we don't know what the  supreme court is going to do you know if  it's if it's a full reversal of roe is  modified by casey i mean this would be a  battle then that was fought at a state  by state level but it's possible the  court does something a little short of  that um but also would be good from our  point of view yeah we absolutely see  that there's you know certain states  like new york and california have you  know very permissive abortion laws in  their states not just limited to the  south like ohio and other places like  that um that would you know do more to  protect unborn children and i think what  we would look for in a lot of states is  really common sense  you know sort of um look at a point  where a woman has an opportunity to know  she's pregnant you know we we  you know you have a set of  exceptions for um you know like physical  health problems that a mother might have  or clearly the life of the mother you  know i think states will do a patchwork  of things and you know that will that  will mean some states you know a woman  will  be in a position to if she's going to  seek abortion she'll need to do it  sooner than if she lived in new york um  so it it if the court goes as far as  overturning row it will return the  states to sort of a patchwork approach  and let's talk about mississippi in  particular which is the subject of  today's hearing there's only one  remaining clinic in mississippi and the  state already had a 20-week limit prior  to this additional 15-week law that's in  question that that already put a lot of  limits on the number of people who were  able to seek abortions in the state um  does it feel to your organization that  that wasn't enough  well the i think you know what happened  is a lot of states were very  enthusiastic when they saw the makeup of  the you know the new makeup of the  supreme court and a lot of states were  you know  hopefully to get laws in in front of the  court i know that the one clinic in  mississippi was doing abortions up to 16  weeks  um and i think the state of mississippi  was trying to assert an interest like  look the abortions at this stage are  actually very dangerous to the woman and  look uh babies at the stage feel pain so  you know  whether or not what the what might be  going on at the time we wanted to be  able to protect these babies in law you  know i mean more clinics couldn't  feasibly open in in the state so it's a  it's it was a point about drawing  attention to the humanity the unborn  child showing these markers where the  state wanted to protect  final question to you jennifer i know  that a fascinating and unprecedented  argument being made in this mississippi  case in front of the supreme court is  that the undue burden thing is is no  longer a valid argument because women  and men are equal women have more access  to capital now according to these  attorneys so they are able to  be moms and care for a child it's not an  undue burden anymore how do you square  that though with the fact that the  majority of women who do seek abortion  services in this country are low income  and don't actually have equal access to  capital  well what i say is i think we we need to  do more work as as pro-lifers to connect  women with pregnancy resources centers  with the resources that they are you  know are able to get through that state  medicaid programs through wic through  you know there's so there's so many  sorts of different resources out there i  think the ability to to  uh put a baby up for adoption is a far  simpler process now than it was when  rose decided and there was a focus today  on the fact that there's so many state  protective laws about if a woman has a  baby she can drop it off no questions  asked there is no sort of ongoing  requirement for the you know for to to  be an active mother of the child um what  we're talking about is protecting sort  of the unborn unborn life  jennifer poppeck legislative director  for national right to life committee  thanks for joining us we're going to  continue to cover this story as we get  updates from the supreme court for now  i'm madison mills in new york

